1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A plugin rarely works by itself, it leans on other plugins, and Cloud Code gives you

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
a way to declare exactly what it needs.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson maps how dependencies get declared, how version ranges resolve, and how enabling

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and disabling cascade through the graph. Let's dive in.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
How does a plugin say what it depends on?

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
It lists those needs in a dependencies array, inside its manifest.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Each entry names another plugin, and Cloud Code reads that list whenever you install.

8
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Here's the payoff.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
When you install a plugin, Cloud Code resolves that array and pulls in every dependency automatically.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
You don't hunt them down by hand, the install output prints exactly what got added, so you

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
can see the full set that landed.

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
I want you to think of a dependency as any other plugin that this one has to have present to function.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,000
This one, and the features you installed the plugin for, simply won't work.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Let's look at what actually goes in that array.

15
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Each entry can be simple or precise.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The simple form is just a bare plugin name, a string that says track whatever the marketplace provides.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
The precise form is an object.

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
You give the name plus a version range, so resolution locks onto a compatible release

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:19,000
instead of drifting.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
That same object can carry one more field, a marketplace name, when the dependency lives

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
in a different catalog than the plugin declaring it.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So think of it as a spectrum, bare when you want to follow the latest, an object when

23
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
a specific version matters, and the marketplace field only when you reach across catalogs.

24
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Most entries stay simple.

25
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Here's the tradeoff between those two forms, side by side.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
With a bare name, you list only the plugin, and it resolves to whatever the marketplace

27
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,000
currently offers.

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
That keeps you current with zero effort.

29
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
But the version you actually get can shift out from under you between installs.

30
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
With a pinned version object, you attach a semver range, something like tilde 2.1.0.

31
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Now clod code fetches the highest tagged release that satisfies your range and nothing newer.

32
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
You trade a little freshness for reproducibility.

33
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Everyone who installs resolves the same compatible version.

34
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
When a plugin's behavior has to stay stable across a team, that predictability is worth pinning for.

35
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Let me make those version ranges concrete, because the syntax carries real meaning.

36
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,000
A tilde like tilde 2.1.0 allows patch updates only.

37
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
You stay on the 2.1 line and pick up bug fixes.

38
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
A caret like caret 2.0 opens things up to minor and patch releases.

39
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:53,000
So you ride new features as long as the major version stays at 2.

40
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
A comparator like greater than or equal 1.4 is the loosest.

41
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
It accepts anything at or above that floor, which gives you the least protection from

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
a breaking change.

43
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Tighter ranges mean more stability.

44
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Looser ranges mean less friction, but more risk.

45
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Enabling pulls dependencies in, but there's a second behavior that trips people up. Enabling.

46
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:24,000
When you enable a plugin, clod code also enables its dependencies, at the same scope, automatically.

47
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And it does this transitively.

48
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
If a dependency has its own dependencies, those come on too, all the way down the chain.

49
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Here's the subtle part.

50
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
A dependency enabled this way is turned on even if its own manifest says it should default to off.

51
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The active plugin that needs it wins.

52
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:46,000
So you rarely flip dependencies on by hand.

53
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
You enable the thing you care about, and everything it relies on wakes up with it as a single working unit.

54
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Now the mirror image, disabling.

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
You might expect to turn any plugin off whenever you like, but the dependency graph steps in.

56
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
If you try to disable a plugin while another enabled plugin still depends on it, clod code

57
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
blocks the action, and it's helpful about it.

58
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The error prints a chained command that disables the dependence first, then the plugin you targeted.

59
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Once nothing enabled points at it anymore, the disable goes through cleanly.

60
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Think about why this matters.

61
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Without that guard, you could silently break a plugin that quietly relied on the one you

62
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
just switched off.

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The graph keeps you from pulling a thread that unravels something else.

64
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:40,000
What happens when two plugins both depend on the same thing, but ask for different versions?

65
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Clod code doesn't just pick one at random.

66
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:47,000
It intersects their ranges and looks for a single version that satisfies every constraint

67
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:52,000
at once, then fetches the highest one that fits.

68
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,000
That's constraint intersection.

69
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:58,000
When the ranges genuinely can't be reconciled, one demands version 1.

70
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Another demands version 3.

71
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
You get a range conflict error, the install fails, and your existing setup is left untouched.

72
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Nothing half applies.

73
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:15,000
And within any single range, resolution always reaches for the newest, tagged release that still fits.

74
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Highest compatible wins.

75
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Understanding that lets you predict exactly which version lands.

76
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Let's close the loop on cleanup.

77
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
When you remove a plugin, the dependencies it dragged in don't leave with it.

78
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
They stay installed because something else might still need them.

79
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Left alone, those become orphans.

80
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Auto-installed plugins with nothing pointing at them anymore.

81
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
To clear them, you run plugin prune.

82
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It sweeps away orphaned auto-installed dependencies and reclaims a clean, minimal set.

83
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Here's the safety guarantee I want you to hold onto.

84
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Prune never removes a plugin you installed directly.

85
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Only the extras that got pulled in automatically and are now unused.

86
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
So you can prune freely without fear of deleting something you chose on purpose. Let's recap.

87
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Declaring dependencies lets Cloud Code assemble a working plugin set for you.

88
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:15,000
It matters because install and enable both cascade automatically, and disable is guarded

89
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
so you can't break a plugin that is depended on by something else.

90
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Just remember, prune clears the orphaned leftovers safely.

91
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
That covers resolving plugin dependencies.

92
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
I'll see you in the next one.

